             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>Let \(f(x) = x^{-p}\), which is decreasing on \([1, N]\). The lower sum for the unit partition is \(L(f, P) = \sum_{k=2}^{N} k^{-p}\), since the infimum on \([k-1, k]\) is \(k^{-p}\). By Definition 1.3 of Lesson 9, \(L(f, P) \le \int_1^N x^{-p}\,dx\). Theorem 2.2 (under Assumption 2.1) gives \(\int_1^N x^{-p}\,dx \le 1/(p-1)\). So \(1 + \sum_{k=2}^{N} k^{-p} \le 1 + 1/(p-1)\), a bound that does not depend on \(N\). The partial sums are nondecreasing and bounded by \(1 + 1/(p-1)\), so, assuming Assumption 2.2, they converge and the series converges.</p>
